# Overlay and contextual interaction contract

Apply these rules to Modal, Drawer, Overlay, Popover, Menu, Tooltip and the primitives they share.

## Dismissal stack

- Escape and outside-pointer dismissal must be coordinated through the shared dismissable-layer stack. Only the top active layer may dismiss in response to a global interaction.
- `closeOnEscape` and `closeOnClickOutside` are independent public behaviors. Wire the matching callback as well as the flag; a boolean prop that never reaches a dismissal handler is incomplete.
- Nested overlay tests must verify that the child closes first and the parent remains open.
- Escape dismissal for Menu and Popover restores focus to the real trigger. Outside-pointer dismissal must not steal focus back from the user's pointer destination.

## Trigger relationships

- Menu and Popover semantics belong on the actual slotted trigger element, not on an internal wrapper.
- Menu triggers expose `aria-haspopup="menu"`, `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`; Popover triggers expose the equivalent dialog relationship and the panel must use the matching stable id.
- Tooltip `aria-describedby` belongs on the slotted trigger root and points to the rendered `role="tooltip"` content while visible.
- Preserve consumer listeners and refs when augmenting a slotted trigger. Do not replace the trigger with a generic wrapper button.

## Menu focus model

- Menu uses roving `tabindex`: one enabled menu item is `0`, all other items are `-1`, and disabled items are never focus targets.
- Trigger `ArrowDown`, `Enter` and `Space` open at the first enabled item; trigger `ArrowUp` opens at the last enabled item.
- Inside the menu, Arrow keys move actual DOM focus, Home/End go to the first/last enabled item, Enter/Space select, and disabled items are skipped.
- Escape closes the current menu and restores focus to its trigger. Selection also returns focus to the trigger unless the public contract explicitly changes.
- Do not implement keyboard navigation as visual `data-active` changes without moving DOM focus.

## Focus

- Modal-like surfaces must trap focus while active and restore focus to the element that opened that specific layer when they close.
- Menu and Popover do not trap focus; they preserve document tab order and restore trigger focus only on dismissal paths that require restoration.
- Nested layers restore focus one level at a time: child trigger first, then parent trigger when the parent closes.
- Tooltip must remain visible when focus moves between descendants of one composite trigger root and close only when focus leaves that root.
- Prefer native focusable controls. Never solve focus trapping by making the entire page inert without proving the nested restoration behavior.

## Scroll locking

- Scroll locking is reference-counted globally but acquisition/release ownership is per hook instance.
- One instance may acquire at most once and release at most once. A false transition followed by unmount must not decrement twice.
- Preserve the body's original inline overflow value when the first lock starts and restore it only after the final lock releases.

## Accessible naming

- Every modal-like element with `role="dialog"` must have an accessible name.
- A visible `title` should generate `aria-labelledby` and takes precedence over `ariaLabel`.
- `ariaLabel` is the explicit titleless-dialog path. Safety fallbacks may prevent unnamed dialogs but must not replace documenting the explicit naming paths.
- Tooltip content is supplementary. Required instructions, errors, validation and critical status must remain visible outside Tooltip.

## Positioning and visual contract

- Public `size`, `position`, `radius`, `centered`, `color`, and `opacity` props must map to observable geometry/state or semantic theme variables.
- Backdrop opacity must affect only the backdrop color, never the overlay's child content.
- Overlay-family layering must use `--dui-z-index-*` theme variables. Do not add literal z-index numbers in component styles.
- Modal and Drawer size scales must cover `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg`, and `xl` with real width changes.
- Document whether a floating component's `position` is a deterministic side or a preferred side with collision flipping/shifting. Never imply collision handling that the implementation does not provide.
- Do not build a custom JavaScript popper or geometry engine. When collision-aware positioning becomes part of the contract, use the project-approved low-level floating-positioning dependency and keep the abstraction doctui-owned.

## Tests and documentation

- Start interaction fixes with failing regression tests before implementation when practical.
- Cover Escape enabled/disabled, outside click enabled/disabled, nested ordering, focus restoration, trigger relationships, menu roving focus, disabled-item skipping, Tooltip hover/focus transitions, reference-counted scroll lock, accessible names, sizing, backdrop tokens and z-index tokens.
- Storybook must include interaction-oriented states and at least one realistic nested composition when multiple overlay surfaces interact. For Menu/Popover/Tooltip work, include keyboard/focus examples rather than visual-only snapshots.
- VitePress and metadata must document the same naming, dismissal, focus, positioning, scroll-lock, sizing and layering contract.
